= Paper 1
{scope}
{title2=General Physics}

Original public paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/natural_science_tripos_physics_e_2/response/2340776/attach/2/FOI%202023%20399%20Zahid%20materials.pdf?cookie_passthrough=1#page=526[Physical PDF pages 526–533]. All ten Section A questions and all eight Section B questions are covered.

= Section A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= A1
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In a <photoelectric effect> experiment, illuminate a clean metal photocathode with monochromatic light and use a retarding voltage to measure the maximum emitted-electron <kinetic energy>. The relation $eV_{\rm stop}=h\nu-\phi$ has a <frequency> threshold and a slope $h$. Increasing intensity chiefly changes the number emitted, while increasing <frequency> increases the available <energy> per emission. This supports <energy> transfer in units $h\nu$, with $\phi$ the <work function>. The retarding-potential measurement distinguishes the <electron> <energy> from the photocurrent; the two respond differently to changes of the light.

= b
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In <electron diffraction>, accelerate <electrons> through a known voltage and measure the angular scattering pattern from a crystal. Distinct <diffraction> maxima occur where the periodic atomic arrangement gives constructive <interference>. Their positions give a wavelength through the crystal spacing and <diffraction> geometry, agreeing with the <de Broglie wavelength> $\lambda=h/p$, with $p\simeq\sqrt{2m_e eV}$ in the nonrelativistic regime. Varying the accelerating voltage shifts the maxima in the predicted way. A localized classical projectile model alone would not explain this wavelength-dependent <interference> pattern.

= A2
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A2}

For independent <Poisson random variables> with known expected counts $\mu_i$, the model variance is $\mu_i$. The <Pearson chi-squared statistic> is therefore
$$
\chi_P^2=\sum_i\frac{(n_i-\mu_i)^2}{\mu_i}
=\frac{121}{20}+\frac{121}{35}+\frac{144}{44}+\frac{49}{36}+\frac{144}{24}+\frac{144}{11}
\simeq\boxed{33.23.}
$$
There are six independently specified bins and no parameters fitted from them, giving approximately six <degrees of freedom>. This is far above its expected value six; the approximate <chi-squared distribution> tail <probability> is $9.5\times10^{-6}$. **The discrepancies are much too large for a good Poisson-noise fit.** Replacing model variances by the observed-count estimates gives the alternative statistic $30.92$, with the same qualitative conclusion. There is no fitted normalization to justify subtracting a degree of freedom or rescaling the model to the observed total.

= A3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A3}

One conduction <electron> per atom gives number density $n=\rho N_A/M\simeq8.50\times10^{28}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. Filling two spin states per <momentum> state in the three-dimensional <Fermi gas> gives $k_F=(3\pi^2n)^{1/3}$ and
$$
\boxed{E_F=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m_e}(3\pi^2n)^{2/3}\simeq7.05\,\mathrm{eV},\qquad
T_F=E_F/k_B\simeq8.18\times10^4\,\mathrm K.}
$$
The <Fermi temperature> greatly exceeds ordinary <temperatures>, explaining why these conduction <electrons> are degenerate rather than a classical Maxwell gas. The numerical result uses the stipulated free-electron band and bare <electron> <mass>, rather than a detailed copper band structure.

= A4
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A4}

For equally nondegenerate levels, the <partition function> is $Z=e^{-\beta\epsilon}(1+e^{-\beta\Delta})$. The mean <energy> per particle is $U=\epsilon+\Delta/(1+e^{\beta\Delta})$, so the <Schottky anomaly> has <heat capacity>
$$
\boxed{C=k_B\left(\frac{\Delta}{k_BT}\right)^2
\frac{e^{\Delta/(k_BT)}}{[1+e^{\Delta/(k_BT)}]^2}.}
$$
At low <temperature>, $C\sim k_B(\Delta/k_BT)^2e^{-\Delta/(k_BT)}$ because excitation is suppressed. At high <temperature>, $C\sim\Delta^2/(4k_BT^2)$ because the two populations approach equality and the <energy> saturates. The peak is about $0.439k_B$ at $k_BT\simeq0.417\Delta$. The common offset $\epsilon$ does not affect <heat capacity>. Multiply by particle number for the total <heat capacity>, or divide by the corresponding <mass> for a mass-specific quantity.

\Image[paper-1-schottky-heat-capacity.png]
{title=Two-level Schottky heat capacity and its low- and high-temperature suppression}

= A5
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A5}

The <infinite square well> levels for width $L$ are $E_n=n^2h^2/(8m_eL^2)$. Starting in the <ground state>, the smallest excitation is $1\to2$, and its electric-dipole matrix element is nonzero. It therefore sets the longest absorption wavelength:
$$
\frac{hc}{\lambda_{\max}}=E_2-E_1=\frac{3h^2}{8m_eL^2},\qquad
\boxed{L=\sqrt{\frac{3h\lambda_{\max}}{8m_ec}}\simeq0.302\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
The corresponding <energy> separation is about $12.4\,\mathrm{eV}$. Elastic scattering of light is a different process from the stipulated absorption into an excited well state.

= A6
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A6}

The quoted angular diameter gives half-angle $\theta=0.005$. At distance $r=10^{13}\,\mathrm m$, the uniform beam covers area $A\simeq\pi(r\theta)^2=7.85\times10^{21}\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Its intensity is $I=P/A\simeq2.93\times10^{-21}\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. For a vacuum electromagnetic wave, $I=\epsilon_0cE_{\rm rms}^2$, so
$$
\boxed{E_{\rm rms}\simeq1.05\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}.}
$$
No extra factor two belongs in this rms relation. The <frequency> fixes the wavelength but is unnecessary once the total power and stipulated beam divergence are specified. This is the ideal directed-beam estimate, before propagation or receiver losses.

= A7
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A7}

Relativistic <synchrotron radiation> is concentrated within angle of order $1/\gamma$ around the instantaneous velocity, tangent to the circular orbit. The narrow instantaneous beam sweeps around with the particle; over a whole orbit the emission forms a thin fan about the orbital plane. With $E\gg m_ec^2$, $\gamma\simeq5000/0.5=10^4$. Since $\nu_{\rm orb}\simeq c/(2\pi R)$, the supplied characteristic-frequency scaling gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda_{\rm char}\sim\frac{2\pi R}{\gamma^3}\simeq6.3\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
This is subnanometer X-ray radiation, with a beaming angle of order $10^{-4}\,\mathrm{rad}$. The conventional critical-angular-frequency normalization $\omega_c=3\gamma^3c/(2R)$ gives $4.2\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m$ instead; the question's order-of-magnitude <frequency> prescription does not distinguish these order-one factors.

\Image[paper-1-synchrotron-beaming.png]
{title=Instantaneous synchrotron beaming along the tangent; opening exaggerated for visibility}

= A8
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A8}

Use local east, north and upward axes. At the equator, Earth's angular velocity points north. To first order in its magnitude $\Omega_E$, the falling object has $v_z=-gt$, so the <Coriolis acceleration> $2\mathbf v\times\boldsymbol\Omega_E$ points east and has magnitude $2\Omega_Egt$. Integrating from zero initial eastward velocity and displacement gives
$$
v_x=\Omega_Egt^2,\qquad x=\frac{\Omega_Eg t^3}{3},\qquad t_f=\sqrt{2h/g}.
$$
For $h=830\,\mathrm m$, $g\simeq9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ and $\Omega_E\simeq7.29\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$,
$$
\boxed{t_f\simeq13.0\,\mathrm s,\qquad x_f\simeq0.525\,\mathrm m\ \text{east of the worker}.}
$$
Local measured $g$ includes the stationary centrifugal correction. Corrections involving the acquired horizontal velocity or the height dependence of effective gravity are beyond this leading estimate. If the printed centrifugal term is read as $+\boldsymbol\Omega\times(\boldsymbol\Omega\times\mathbf r)$, its sign is opposite the usual outward inertial <force>; it does not affect the retained first-order Coriolis result.

= A9
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A9}

The transverse-wave impedance of a string is $Z=\sqrt{T\mu}$ for common tension $T$. The density ratio four gives $Z_2/Z_1=2$. Continuity of displacement and transverse <force> gives transmitted amplitude ratio $t=2Z_1/(Z_1+Z_2)=2/3$. The <power transmission coefficient> includes the impedance ratio:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T=\frac{Z_2}{Z_1}|t|^2=\frac89.}
$$
The reflected fraction is $1/9$, checking <energy> conservation. Squaring the displacement amplitude alone would give the wrong transmitted power.

= A10
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A10}

For <photons> emitted back to back in the measurement frame, their total invariant <mass> obeys $m^2c^4=(E_1+E_2)^2-(E_1-E_2)^2=4E_1E_2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{E_2=\frac{(125\,\mathrm{GeV})^2}{4(30\,\mathrm{GeV})}\simeq130.2\,\mathrm{GeV}.}
$$
The parent's total laboratory <energy> is consequently about $160.2\,\mathrm{GeV}$, exceeding its rest <energy>. The measured-frame opposite-directions condition is essential: opposite directions only in the parent's rest frame would hold automatically and would not determine the second laboratory <energy> by itself.

= Section B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= B1
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B1}

In a central potential, $J=mr^2\dot\theta$ is conserved. Substituting it into $U=m(\dot r^2+r^2\dot\theta^2)/2+V(r)$ gives
$$
U=\frac m2\dot r^2+V_{\rm eff}(r),\qquad V_{\rm eff}=\frac{J^2}{2mr^2}+V(r).
$$
While the string stays taut, $V=k(r-L)^2/2$. Circular <force> balance gives $mr_0\omega_0^2=k(r_0-L)$, hence $\omega_0^2=k(r_0-L)/(mr_0)$. A radial impulse changes no <angular momentum>. The <radial oscillation of an elastic-string orbit> follows by expanding the same fixed-$J$ effective potential about its minimum:
$$
m\ddot{\delta r}=-V_{\rm eff}''(r_0)\delta r,\quad
\Omega^2=\frac{k}{m}+3\omega_0^2=\frac{k}{m}\left(4-\frac{3L}{r_0}\right),
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\frac\Omega{\omega_0}=\sqrt{\frac{4r_0-3L}{r_0-L}}.}
$$
For an outward impulse, one convenient linear approximation is $r=r_0+\epsilon\sin\Omega t$ and $\theta=\omega_0t+[2\omega_0\epsilon/(r_0\Omega)](\cos\Omega t-1)$. The angular modulation follows from $\dot\theta=J/(mr^2)$ and is needed for a consistent trajectory sketch.

\Image[paper-1-elastic-string-orbits.png]
{title=Small radial-impulse trajectories: threefold modulation and the harmonic-orbit limit}

= a
{parent=B1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At $r_0=6L/5$, the <frequency> ratio is $\Omega/\omega_0=3$. There are three radial oscillations during one mean revolution, giving a nearly circular orbit with three outward and three inward extrema. The leading small-amplitude sketch closes after one revolution. Finite-amplitude corrections generally change the <frequency> ratio and can produce apsidal drift; the linear ratio does not prove an exactly closed finite-amplitude orbit for the full stretched-string potential.

= b
{parent=B1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $r_0\gg L$, $\Omega/\omega_0\to2$. The potential approaches the isotropic harmonic-oscillator form $kr^2/2$, and the perturbed path is approximately a small-eccentricity ellipse centered on the fixed point. The radius has two maxima and two minima per revolution. For finite nonzero $L/r_0$, the ellipse is an approximation and slow precession can remain.

= B2
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B2}

Expand the propagation distance at small angles:
$$
r\simeq L+\frac{x_0^2+y_0^2}{2L}-\frac{x_0x+y_0y}{L}+\frac{x^2+y^2}{2L}.
$$
The first two terms supply an aperture-independent phase. If the aperture quadratic phase is small, $k(x^2+y^2)/(2L)\ll1$, it too may be dropped from the integrand. The remaining <Fraunhofer diffraction> amplitude is the aperture's <Fourier transform> at transverse wavevector $(kx_0/L,ky_0/L)$, as required.

For slit width $w$ and center spacing $d$, adding the two shifted top-hat transforms gives the normalized intensity
$$
\boxed{\frac{I(X)}{I(0)}=\left[\frac{\sin(\pi wX/(\lambda L))}{\pi wX/(\lambda L)}\right]^2
\cos^2\left(\frac{\pi dX}{\lambda L}\right).}
$$
The aperture outer half-width is $17.5\,\mathrm{\mu m}$; its maximum quadratic phase is only about $1.5\times10^{-3}$ radians at the given wavelength and distance, so the Fraunhofer approximation is excellent. Fringe spacing is $\lambda L/d=25.28\,\mathrm{mm}$ and the first single-slit envelope zero is at $\lambda L/w=63.2\,\mathrm{mm}$.

\Image[paper-1-double-slit-photon-counting.png]
{title=Two finite-width slits: interference fringes, diffraction envelope and shifted side maximum}

The <side maximum of a finite-width double-slit pattern> shifts slightly inward from $25.28\,\mathrm{mm}$. Differentiating the full intensity gives, away from zeros,
$$
\frac{\pi w}{\lambda L}\left[\cot u-\frac1u\right]
=\frac{\pi d}{\lambda L}\tan v,\quad u=\frac{\pi wX}{\lambda L},\quad v=\frac{\pi dX}{\lambda L}.
$$
Its first side maximum is at $X\simeq23.88\,\mathrm{mm}$ with $I/I(0)\simeq0.592$. The expected rate is therefore $59.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Poisson shot noise gives signal-to-noise ratio $N/\sqrt N=\sqrt N$, so one needs $N=100$ counts:
$$
\boxed{t\simeq100/(59.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}})=1.69\,\mathrm s.}
$$
Using the common fringe-center approximation instead gives relative intensity $\operatorname{sinc}^2(\pi w/d)\simeq0.573$ and $t\simeq1.75\,\mathrm s$. Both express the same order of exposure; the first uses the true maximum. Dark counts, background subtraction and uncertainty in the reference rate are excluded by the ideal stated noise model.

= B3
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B3}

An ideal linear polarizer projects onto $\mathbf e_\theta=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)^T$. Thus its <Jones matrix> is $P_\theta=\mathbf e_\theta\mathbf e_\theta^T$, immediately giving the specified entries. For time convention $e^{-i\omega t}$, a birefringent plate of thickness $d$ produces relative phase $2\pi(n_y-n_x)d/\lambda$. A quarter-wave thickness $d=\lambda/[4(n_y-n_x)]$ gives, after removing a common phase, $Q=\operatorname{diag}(1,i)$ when $x$ is fast.

For incident linear state $\mathbf E=E_0(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)^T$, the original order gives
$$
QP_{\pi/4}\mathbf E=\frac{E_0}{2}(\cos\theta+\sin\theta)\binom1i,
\qquad\boxed{I/I_0=\cos^2(\theta-\pi/4).}
$$
The transmitted state is circular wherever its amplitude is nonzero. Reversing the components gives
$$
P_{\pi/4}Q\mathbf E=\frac{E_0}{2}(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)\binom11,
\qquad\boxed{I/I_0=\frac12,}
$$
and the output is linearly polarized at $45^\circ$. The incident circular state $\mathbf E\propto(1,i)^T$, called left-circular in the paper's convention, becomes $(1,-1)^T$ after $Q$ and is extinguished by $P_{\pi/4}$. Explicit Jones vectors fix the handedness convention without relying on an ambiguous viewing direction.

\Image[paper-1-polarizer-order.png]
{title=Transmitted intensity versus incident linear polarization for the two element orders}

With the stated normal mirror after the original system, an arbitrary incident field is first projected onto $45^\circ$ and then made circular. In fixed laboratory transverse axes, the mirror contributes a common reflection phase, and the reciprocal return plate contributes another $Q$. The round-trip matrix is consequently proportional to
$$
P_{\pi/4}Q^2P_{\pi/4}=P_{\pi/4}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}P_{\pi/4}=0.
$$
The returned field is orthogonal to the polarizer and is blocked. This suppresses the stipulated specular return toward a source, useful for avoiding optical feedback. The result concerns that mirror and return geometry; a reciprocal circular-polarizer assembly does not provide the general nonreciprocal two-port property of a Faraday optical isolator.

= B4
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B4}

<Configurational entropy> counts the arrangements compatible with a macroscopic configuration: $S=k_B\ln\Omega$. Let $x=L/(Na)$, $N_\downarrow=N(1+x)/2$ and $N_\uparrow=N(1-x)/2$. The multiplicity is $N!/(N_\downarrow!N_\uparrow!)$. <Stirling's approximation> therefore gives
$$
S=Nk_B\left[\ln2-\frac{1+x}{2}\ln(1+x)-\frac{1-x}{2}\ln(1-x)\right],\qquad |x|\le1.
$$
With downward $L$ positive, the gravitational <potential energy> is $-mgL$. Minimizing the <Helmholtz free energy> $F=-mgL-TS$ gives $\operatorname{artanh}x=mga/(k_BT)$, hence
$$
\boxed{L=Na\tanh\left(\frac{mga}{k_BT}\right).}
$$
Raising <temperature> reduces the extension: the entropic preference for many up/down arrangements opposes alignment by the load. For small loads, $L\simeq Na^2mg/(k_BT)$.

For the <positive-feedback entropic chain> with replacement <force> $f(L)=f_0L/a$, $f_0>0$, the conservative potential is $U(L)=-\int_0^Lf(s)\,ds=-f_0L^2/(2a)$. Using $-f(L)L$ would overcount it by a factor two. The stationary equation becomes
$$
\boxed{x=\tanh\left(\frac{T_c}{T}x\right),\qquad T_c=\frac{Naf_0}{k_B}.}
$$
Expansion about $x=0$ gives
$$
F=F(0)+\frac{Nk_B}{2}(T-T_c)x^2+\frac{Nk_BT}{12}x^4+O(x^6).
$$
For $T>T_c$ the stable extension is zero. Below $T_c$, nonzero minima appear, with $x\simeq\pm\sqrt{3(T_c-T)/T}$ close to the transition and $|x|\to1$ as $T\to0$. Restricting to downward extensions selects the positive branch. **The onset is continuous in this model**, with a nonanalytic mean-field change at $T_c$; a finite jump in $L$ does not follow from the printed <force> law, despite the question's discontinuity wording. A finite chain has rounded fluctuations rather than a literal thermodynamic singularity.

\Image[paper-1-chain-mean-field-transition.png]
{title=Positive-feedback chain: continuous mean-field extension and changing free-energy minima}

This is the same self-consistency equation as the zero-field mean-field treatment of the <Ising model>: the segment orientation corresponds to a spin, extension to magnetization, and the extension-dependent <force> to the self-consistent molecular field. The competition between alignment <energy> and binary configurational <entropy> explains the shared <temperature> dependence.

= B5
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B5}

In the $S_z$ basis $\alpha,\beta$, $S_x=(\hbar/2)\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Its normalized eigenstates are $(\alpha+\beta)/\sqrt2$ and $(\alpha-\beta)/\sqrt2$, with eigenvalues $+\hbar/2$ and $-\hbar/2$. Expanding the initial state in this basis and evolving the two phases gives, with $\Omega=\lambda B$,
$$
\boxed{|\psi(t)\rangle=\cos(\Omega t/2)\,\alpha-i\sin(\Omega t/2)\,\beta,\qquad
P(S_z=+\hbar/2)=\cos^2(\Omega t/2).}
$$
The sign of $\Omega$ fixes rotation sense; the <probability> is insensitive to that sign.

In the <Stern-Gerlach experiment>, a collimated atomic beam passes through a strongly nonuniform <magnetic field>. The <force> component $F_z\simeq\mu_z\partial_zB_z$ separates magnetic-moment projections spatially. Silver atoms provide an effective spin-half moment, yielding two resolved components rather than a continuous distribution of deflections. To test the rotation above, use a first analyzer and aperture to prepare $+z$, pass that selected beam through a controlled approximately uniform $x$-directed field, and analyze it with a second $z$-oriented Stern-Gerlach magnet. Varying field strength or interaction time changes the two output populations as the calculated cosine and sine squares. A narrow velocity distribution and controlled field-entry geometry preserve the contrast by reducing variations of interaction time and unwanted spin evolution.

= B6
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B6}

For charge per length $\lambda_q$, <Gauss's law> gives $E_r=\lambda_q/(2\pi\epsilon_0r)$ between the conductors. Integration gives $V=\lambda_q\ln(b/a)/(2\pi\epsilon_0)$. Similarly <Ampère's law> gives $B_\phi=\mu_0I/(2\pi r)$; integrating the magnetic <energy> per length and setting it equal to $LI^2/2$ gives
$$
\boxed{C=\frac{2\pi\epsilon_0}{\ln(b/a)},\qquad L=\frac{\mu_0}{2\pi}\ln(b/a).}
$$
Here $C,L$ are per unit length. The displayed $L$ is the external TEM inductance, appropriate to the ideal surface-current/high-frequency model. Internal magnetic <energy> in conductors with appreciable current penetration adds internal inductance; it cannot be determined fully from an outer conductor with unspecified wall geometry.

The distributed series resistance and inductance, with shunt capacitance and negligible leakage, give the <telegrapher's equations>
$$
\partial_xV=-L\partial_tI-RI,\qquad\partial_xI=-C\partial_tV.
$$
Differentiate the first with respect to $x$ and substitute the second to obtain
$$
\boxed{\partial_x^2V=LC\partial_t^2V+RC\partial_tV.}
$$
For $V\propto e^{ikx-i\omega t}$ the forward attenuating branch has $k^2=\omega^2LC+i\omega RC$. When $R\ll\omega L$,
$$
\boxed{k\simeq\omega\sqrt{LC}+i\frac R2\sqrt{\frac CL},\qquad
v_{\rm ph}\simeq\frac1{\sqrt{LC}}=c,\qquad
\ell_{\rm amp}=\frac2R\sqrt{\frac LC}.}
$$
The positive imaginary part gives amplitude $e^{-x/\ell_{\rm amp}}$. Power decays twice as fast in its exponent, so its attenuation length is $\ell_{\rm amp}/2$. Finite conductivity changes the speed only at higher order in this constant-parameter weak-loss expansion, apart from any separately retained internal inductance or dielectric response.

= B7
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B7}

The <proper time> satisfies $dt/d\tau=\gamma(u)$. The collinear transformation from the Earth frame into the instantaneous rest frame gives proper acceleration $f=\gamma^3du/dt$, so
$$
\frac{du}{d\tau}=\frac{f}{\gamma^2}=f\left(1-\frac{u^2}{c^2}\right).
$$
Starting from rest, integration gives $\operatorname{artanh}(u/c)=f\tau/c$. Hence
$$
\boxed{u=c\tanh(f\tau/c),\qquad t=\frac cf\sinh(f\tau/c).}
$$
Since $dx/d\tau=u\gamma=c\sinh(f\tau/c)$, a further integration gives
$$
\boxed{x(\tau)=\frac{c^2}{f}\left[\cosh(f\tau/c)-1\right].}
$$
Using $\tau=10$ Julian years and $f=1\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ gives $f\tau/c\simeq1.053$, so the star is **about $5.77$ light years away** in the Earth frame. The Earth-frame travel time is about $11.95$ years and the final speed is about $0.783c$. The model accelerates toward the star throughout; adding a braking leg would be a different itinerary.

= B8
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B8}

Substitution of a plane wave in the <Schrödinger equation> gives $\hbar\omega=\hbar^2k^2/(2m)+V_0$. For a stationary step-scattering state, remove the common phase $e^{-iEt/\hbar}$ and write $\psi_L=e^{ikx}+r e^{-ikx}$, $k=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$. If $E>V_0$, the right-side outgoing wave is $\psi_R=t e^{iqx}$, $q=\sqrt{2m(E-V_0)}/\hbar$. A finite step with no delta-function potential in the Schrödinger operator requires continuous $\psi$ and $\psi'$: a discontinuous <wavefunction> would generate a delta derivative, and integrating the equation over a vanishing interval gives derivative continuity. Matching yields
$$
r=\frac{k-q}{k+q},\qquad t=\frac{2k}{k+q},\qquad
\boxed{\mathcal R=\left(\frac{\sqrt E-\sqrt{E-V_0}}{\sqrt E+\sqrt{E-V_0}}\right)^2\quad(E>V_0).}
$$
For $0<E<V_0$, take $q=i\kappa$ and the bounded right-side state $te^{-\kappa x}$. It carries no transmitted flux and $\mathcal R=1$. The threshold limit also has reflection <probability> one. Thus at $V_0=1\,\mathrm{eV}$,
$$
\boxed{\mathcal R(0.9\,\mathrm{eV})=1,\qquad\mathcal R(1.1\,\mathrm{eV})\simeq0.288.}
$$
For the two-step profile, set $k_0=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$, $k_1=\sqrt{2m(E-V_1)}/\hbar$ and $k_2=\sqrt{2m(E-V_0)}/\hbar$. With all regions propagating, the net reflection amplitude is
$$
r_{\rm tot}=\frac{r_{01}+r_{12}e^{2ik_1a}}{1+r_{01}r_{12}e^{2ik_1a}},\qquad
r_{ij}=\frac{k_i-k_j}{k_i+k_j}.
$$
The given quarter-wave condition makes $e^{2ik_1a}=-1$. Zero reflection then requires $r_{01}=r_{12}$, equivalent to $k_1^2=k_0k_2$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{E-V_1=\sqrt{E(E-V_0)},\qquad\text{or}\qquad E=\frac{V_1^2}{2V_1-V_0}.}
$$
For an increasing intermediate step this needs $E>V_0$ and $V_0/2<V_1<V_0$, together with the stipulated thickness condition. In this <quarter-wave matching layer for a quantum potential step>, two reflected amplitudes cancel while the transmitted flux equals the incident flux.
